  "notes": "So So Def girl group carrying Jermaine Dupri's Atlanta hip-hop soul with heavy quartet harmony, 1993–1998. The research reads \"57–70 half-time ballads (reads 114–140) + 79–102 straight\" and names the 57 reading as \"the slowest sustained band on this roster\", so the felt half-time spine is the base with the mode at 63. The three drum deltas are the entry's own words: kick \"on 1 and the 8th before 3\" at one or two hits a bar with the genre's syncopation nearly switched off, \"snare/clap on beat 3 of the doubled grid\", and \"hats either absent or 16ths at low velocity\" — a non-continuous 16th lane at a fifth of the parent's fill density. \"No swing on the ballads\" is stated outright, so `swing.amount` is 0.50 rather than the genre's 0.62. Harmony is \"two- and four-chord minor-7th vamps, Rhodes or clean electric piano as the chord instrument, no countermelody\", encoded as seventh-weighted two-chord families with the counter pushed to a thin pad. \"Melisma reserved for the tag\" and the block harmony explain the low melodic density and the high chord-tone bias. ⚠ Confidence is medium because the research rates it medium and marks the specific keys `[LOW-CONF]`; the keys here are therefore weighted toward the lane's minor centre rather than to named tonics. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all velocity, density and register numbers and the bass block — \"sustained synth bass holding the root, mixed forward\" is a direction, read here as a near-1.0 root-follow with a single-degree cell. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode ballads\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
